<TopicPage slug="subjects/film/intermediate/sound-design-and-score">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Sound is <strong>half the film you never watch</strong>: the grammar lesson\'s "sound is half
    of cinema", delivered — designed, performed and mixed. Bad sound reads instantly as amateur;
    great sound is invisible and does the emotional lifting.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the layers, Foley, off-screen space, score strategy" widget="equationStep" config={soundCfg} />

  <h2>Landmarks and techniques</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ben Burtt\'s Star Wars library:</strong> the lightsaber hum (a TV-set interference plus a film projector), R2-D2\'s voice (synth plus human) — the lesson that signature sounds are INVENTED, engineered characters (the timbre lesson\'s sound design, cinematic edition).</li>
    <li><strong>Psycho\'s shower scene:</strong> screeching violins as violence — Bernard Herrmann scored a scene Hitchcock thought didn\'t need music; the most famous sound-image fusion ever, arguing forever for the composer\'s seat.</li>
    <li><strong>Walter Murch\'s sound montage (Apocalypse Now):</strong> the helicopter-blade/fan match — sound leads the cut; the editing lesson\'s bridges composed forward by audio.</li>
    <li><strong>The loudness trap:</strong> modern mixes (and trailers) compress dynamics for impact — dynamic range (whisper to thunder) is the resource; exhausting an audience in 40 minutes is bad economics for the drama (the music lesson\'s dynamics, industrialised).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Murch on sound design; Sonnenschein, Sound Design; the grammar and music lessons of this atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
